      "math_academy_id": "e-14367",
      "problem": "Which of the following lines is parallel to $12x - y=3?$\n\nI. $y=4x-8$\n\nII. $y=12x+1$\n\nIII. $y=3x-12$",
      "worked_solution": "For two lines to be parallel, they must have the same slope.\n\nTo find the slope of the line $12x-y=3,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-y & =3 \\\\ -y & =-12x+3 \\\\ y & =12x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope is $12.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only line that has a slope of $12$ is $y = 12x+1.$",
